wing them exposes their  bad ideas  i believe that the responsible approach  is what stephen bennell ii  also known to some of you as destiny in  the online gaming world  calls responsible platforming which  means  if you interview the racists or the  extremists or whoever you have to  understand  it's not always obvious to the audience  that their ideas are bad  racist discriminatory ideas they want to  do the interview  because they want any platform to be  used  to get these ideas out there and maybe  someone will be convinced by their ideas  so  simply saying to them tell me why you're  a racist  tell me why jews are the problem in all  of the world whatever  and having them tell you why that  doesn't expose them that's not  responsible platforming responsible  platforming  means you're prepared to challenge their  ideas not in a screaming and yelling way  we don't yell and scream on this program  but with thoughtful well-crafted  questions where you've researched what  their likely responses are going to be  so that you can interrupt their use of  your platform for propaganda purposes  that's what i try to do  i don't think i always succeed pat at  least not as well as i want to  but there's this middle ground between  they should be completely de-platformed  and we pretend their ideas don't exist  that doesn't work because that actually  can provide cover for their ideas  to to catalyze and to metastasize the  other side is  just interviewing them is bad for them  because it exposes their bad ideas  responsible platforming is the path  forward yeah i mean i think in most  corporate outlets the reporters will  either try to go for the gotcha question  to catch them in like some sound bite  that's not going to play out well for  them sure  or they don't know which questions to  ask and they don't know how to push them  effectively because  they're not used to asking very pressing  questions if they're interviewing a  politician they don't want to uh  say somethi